<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ial"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ial.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iâl</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Iâl, anglicised as Yale, was a medieval commote in north-east Wales, around Llanarmon-yn-Iâl and the Alyn valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/iamblichus-i-of-emesa"><span class="enc-name">Iamblichus I of Emesa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Iamblichus I was a ruler of Emesa in Syria, son of Sampsigeramus I, who supported Julius Caesar and other Roman commanders during the civil wars of the 40s and 30s BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ian-smith.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ian Smith</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ian Smith was a Rhodesian Front politician who became prime minister of Southern Rhodesia in 1964.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iao-valley"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iao-valley.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">ʻĪao Valley</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">ʻĪao Valley is a steep, lush valley in the West Maui Mountains above Wailuku, Hawaii, known for the ʻĪao Needle, a rock pinnacle.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iapygians"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iapygians.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iapygians</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iapygians were the ancient peoples of Apulia in south-eastern Italy, divided into the Daunians, Peucetians and Messapians, who spoke the Messapic language.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iarbas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iarbas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iarbas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Iarbas was a legendary Berber king in North Africa associated with the founding of Carthage.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/iaudas"><span class="enc-name">Iaudas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Iaudas was a Berber king of the Aurès mountains in the 530s who resisted the Byzantine reconquest of North Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/iazyges"><span class="enc-name">Iazyges</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iazyges were a Sarmatian people who settled on the Great Hungarian Plain between the Danube and the Tisza in the early first century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibadan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibadan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibadan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibadan is a city in south-western Nigeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibadism"><span class="enc-name">Ibadism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibadism is a branch of Islam distinct from both Sunnism and Shiism, which arose in Basra in the late 7th century and is named after the early figure Abd Allah ibn Ibad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibak-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibak-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibak Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibak Khan was a Shaybanid ruler of the Tyumen Khanate in western Siberia, whose people paid tribute in furs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iban-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iban-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iban people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iban are an indigenous people of Borneo, the largest ethnic group of Sarawak in Malaysia, also living in West Kalimantan and Brunei.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibaraki-prefecture"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibaraki-prefecture.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibaraki Prefecture</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibaraki Prefecture is a prefecture of Japan in the Kanto region, north-east of Tokyo, with its capital at Mito.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibarra"><span class="enc-name">Ibarra</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibarra is a city in northern Ecuador, capital of Imbabura Province, in the Andes north of Quito.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibbi-sin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibbi-Sin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibbi-Sin was the last king of the Third Dynasty of Ur, reigning about 2028–2004 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-zikir"><span class="enc-name">Ibbi-Zikir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibbi-Zikir was a vizier of the kingdom of Ebla in the twenty-fourth century BCE, the son and successor of the vizier Ibrium.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibbit-lim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibbit-lim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibbit-Lim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibbit-Lim was a king of Ebla in the early second millennium BCE, when the city had revived under Amorite rule.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iberia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iberia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iberia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Iberia, or the Iberian Peninsula, is the south-western peninsula of Europe, comprising Spain and Portugal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iberian-union"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iberian-union.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iberian Union</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iberian Union was the dynastic union of the crowns of Portugal and Spain under the Spanish Habsburg kings, beginning in 1580 and lasting until 1640.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iberians"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iberians.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iberians</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iberians were the pre-Roman peoples of the eastern and southern Iberian Peninsula, among them the Turdetani, Edetani and Ilergetes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibibio-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibibio-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibibio people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ibibio are a people of south-eastern Nigeria, living mainly in Akwa Ibom State between the Cross River and the coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibini-ukpabi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibini-ukpabi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibini Ukpabi</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibini Ukpabi was an oracle shrine at Arochukwu in south-eastern Nigeria, known to Europeans as the Long Juju.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibiranu"><span class="enc-name">Ibiranu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibiranu was king of Ugarit in the later thirteenth century BCE, the son of Ammishtamru II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/iblul-il"><span class="enc-name">Iblul-Il</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Iblul-Il was a king of Mari on the Euphrates in the twenty-fourth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-abbar"><span class="enc-name">Ibn al-Abbar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn al-Abbar was an Andalusi scholar, poet and historian from Valencia who served as secretary to its Muslim rulers and, after the city fell to Aragon in 1238, to the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-ashath"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-al-ashath.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn al-Ash'ath</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn al-Ash'ath was an Arab nobleman of Kufa who led a great revolt against the Umayyad governor al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf from 700 to 703.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-muqarrab"><span class="enc-name">Ibn al-Muqarrab</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn al-Muqarrab al-Uyuni was an Arabic poet from al-Ahsa in eastern Arabia and a member of the Uyunid ruling family.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-thumna"><span class="enc-name">Ibn al-Thumna</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn al-Thumna was one of the rival Muslim lords who divided Sicily in the 1050s, ruling Syracuse and Catania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-ammar"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Ammar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Ammar was an Andalusi poet from Silves and the friend and vizier of al-Mutamid of Seville.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-arabi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Arabi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Arabi was an Andalusian Sufi mystic, philosopher and poet, born in Murcia in 1165, who became one of the most influential thinkers in Islamic history.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-arafa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-arafa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Arafa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Arafa was a jurist of the Maliki school from Tunis, the leading religious scholar of the Hafsid capital in the fourteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-battuta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Battuta</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Battuta was a fourteenth-century Moroccan traveller whose account of his journeys across Africa and Asia is a major historical source.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-bibi"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Bibi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Bibi was a Persian historian of the Sultanate of Rum in the 13th century, a court official who served the Seljuk sultans under their Mongol overlords.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hajar-al-asqalani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-hajar-al-asqalani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani was an Egyptian scholar of hadith and Shafi'i jurist of Mamluk Cairo, where he served several terms as chief judge.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hamdin"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Hamdin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Hamdin was the qadi of Córdoba, from a leading family of Maliki jurists, who was proclaimed emir by the city's notables in 1144 during the revolts against the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-hawqal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Hawqal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Hawqal was a tenth-century Arab geographer and traveller.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawshab"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Hawshab</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Hawshab (died 914), known as Mansur al-Yaman, was the founder of the Isma'ili mission in Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hud"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-hud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Hud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Hud, Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Hud al-Mutawakkil, claimed descent from the Hudids of Zaragoza and rose against the Almohads in 1228 in the Ricote valley near Murcia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-isfandiyar"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Isfandiyar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Isfandiyar (Baha al-Din Muhammad ibn Hasan) was a Persian historian of Tabaristan, the Caspian province of northern Iran, who wrote his Tarikh-i Tabaristan around…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-jahhaf"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Jahhaf</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Jahhaf was the qadi of Valencia who in 1092 led a rising that killed al-Qadir, the Castilian client ruler of the city, and appealed to the Almoravids for help.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-khaldun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Khaldun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Khaldun was an Arab historian and thinker born in Tunis in 1332 to a family of Andalusi origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khallikan"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Khallikan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Khallikan was an Arab scholar and judge born in Erbil, who served as chief qadi of Damascus under the Mamluks.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-mahfuz"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Mahfuz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Mahfuz, Shuayb ibn Muhammad ibn Mahfuz, made himself independent ruler of Niebla and its district around 1234 as Ibn Hud's power faded.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-mardanish"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Mardanish</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Mardanish, called the Wolf King by Christian writers, ruled Murcia and Valencia in eastern al-Andalus from 1147 until his death.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-maymun"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-maymun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Maymun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Maymun was the leading figure of a family of admirals who had commanded the Almoravid fleet at Almería, the chief port and naval base of al-Andalus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-muljam"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-muljam.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Muljam</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Rahman ibn Muljam was a Kharijite who assassinated the fourth caliph, Ali ibn Abi Talib, in the mosque of Kufa in January 661, striking him with a poisoned sword…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-qasi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-qasi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Qasi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Qasi was a Sufi leader of the Algarve who led the Muridin revolt against the Almoravids in 1144, seizing Mértola and Silves and ruling as an independent lord.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-rashiq"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Rashiq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Rashiq was an officer of the poet-vizier Ibn Ammar who took over the rule of Murcia in the early 1080s, defying al-Mutamid of Seville.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-rusta"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Rusta</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Rusta was a Persian geographer of the early 10th century, probably from Isfahan, author of the Book of Precious Records.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-salim-al-aswani"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Salim al-Aswani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Salim al-Aswani was an Egyptian official and writer of the 10th century, sent by the Fatimid commander Jawhar as envoy to the king of Makuria around 969.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-saud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Saud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Saud, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Rahman Al Saud, recaptured Riyadh from the Rashidis in 1902 and rebuilt Saudi power in central Arabia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-sulaym-al-aswani"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Sulaym al-Aswani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Sulaym al-Aswani was a 10th-century Egyptian official and writer, sent around 969 by the Fatimid commander Jawhar as envoy to the Christian king of Makuria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tafrakin"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Tafrakin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Tafrakin was a chamberlain, or hajib, of the Hafsid sultanate and the dominant figure in Tunis in the mid-fourteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tayfur"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Tayfur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Tayfur was a local leader who made Mértola, an old river port on the Guadiana in southern Portugal, independent around 1033.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-tufayl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Tufayl</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Tufayl was an Andalusian philosopher, physician and court official of the Almohad Caliphate, born near Granada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-tumart.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Tumart</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Tumart was a Berber religious reformer who preached a strict doctrine of the unity of God and proclaimed himself the Mahdi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-wafid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-wafid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Wafid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Wafid (1008–1074) was an Andalusi physician, pharmacologist and agronomist at the court of al-Mamun of Toledo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-zamrak"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Zamrak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Zamrak was a poet and vizier at the Nasrid court of Granada under Muhammad V.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-zaydun"><span class="enc-name">Ibn Zaydun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibn Zaydun (1003–1071) was an Arab poet of Córdoba, regarded as one of the greatest poets of al-Andalus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibo-island"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibo-island.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibo Island</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibo is a small island of the Quirimbas archipelago off Cabo Delgado province in northern Mozambique.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adeer"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Adeer</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Adeer was the founder of the Gobroon dynasty of the Geledi Sultanate in southern Somalia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-i"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Adil Shah I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Adil Shah I ruled the Bijapur Sultanate from 1534 to 1558, after the brief reign of his brother Mallu Adil Shah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-adil-shah-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Adil Shah II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Adil Shah II was Sultan of Bijapur from 1580 to 1627, under whom the Deccan sultanate reached its greatest extent and prosperity.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-al-hamdi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-al-hamdi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim al-Hamdi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim al-Hamdi (1943–1977) was president of the Yemen Arab Republic (North Yemen) from 1974 to 1977.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-dabo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-dabo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Dabo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Dabo was Emir of Kano from 1819 to 1846, the second ruler of the emirate created by the Fulani jihad within the Sokoto Caliphate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-heski"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-heski.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Heski</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Heski was a Kurdish tribal leader of the Jalali in the Mount Ararat region.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim I ibn al-Aghlab</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim I ibn al-Aghlab was an Arab army commander who founded the Aghlabid dynasty of Ifriqiya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-ibn-hajjaj"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim ibn Hajjaj</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim ibn Hajjaj was an Arab noble of Seville who made the city an autonomous lordship during the fitna that shook the Emirate of Córdoba in the late 9th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-ibn-hamushk"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim ibn Hamushk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim ibn Hamushk was a commander in al-Andalus who seized Jaén and the castles of the Sierra de Segura during the collapse of Almoravid rule after 1145.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-ii-of-ifriqiya"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-ii-of-ifriqiya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim II of Ifriqiya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim II was the Aghlabid emir of Ifriqiya from 875 to 902.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-ii-of-karaman"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-ii-of-karaman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim II of Karaman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim II was ruler of the Karamanid beylik in south-central Anatolia from 1424 to 1464.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-iskandar-of-johor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-iskandar-of-johor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Iskandar of Johor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Iskandar is the Sultan of Johor, who succeeded his father in 2010, and the King of Malaysia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Khalil Khan was khan of Karabakh from 1763, ruling from the fortress of Shusha with the poet Molla Panah Vagif as his vizier.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khan-of-lar"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Khan of Lar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Khan was the last ruler of the Kingdom of Lar, a Sunni principality in southern Persia controlling trade routes to the Persian Gulf.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-lodi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Lodi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Lodi was the last sultan of the Lodi dynasty and of the Delhi Sultanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-nagwamatse"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Nagwamatse</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Nagwamatse was Emir of Kontagora from 1880, son of the emirate's founder Umaru Nagwamatse.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-nasir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-nasir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Nasir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Nasir was prime minister of the Maldives under the sultanate from 1957.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-njoya"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-njoya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Njoya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Njoya was king of Bamum in western Cameroon from the late 1880s until 1931.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-of-darfur"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim of Darfur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim was a sultan of Darfur who died in 1874.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-pasha-baban"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-pasha-baban.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Pasha Baban</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Pasha Baban was a ruler of the Baban emirate, a Kurdish principality in the mountains of Shahrizor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-pasha-of-egypt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-pasha-of-egypt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt was the eldest son of Muhammad Ali and the chief commander of his armies.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-rauza"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-rauza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Rauza</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ibrahim Rauza is a tomb and mosque complex at Bijapur in Karnataka, India, built for Sultan Ibrahim Adil Shah II of the Bijapur Sultanate and his queen and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-shah"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Shah was a nephew of Nader Shah and briefly a ruler of the Afsharid state.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-shah-sharqi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-shah-sharqi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Shah Sharqi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Shah Sharqi was Sultan of Jaunpur from 1402 to 1440, the greatest ruler of the Sharqi dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-tamghach-khan"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Tamghach Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Tamghach Khan, son of Nasr ibn Ali, took Transoxiana when the Kara-Khanid Khanate divided around 1042 and founded the Western Kara-Khanid Khanate with its…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-zaki"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibrahim-zaki.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahim Zaki</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahim Zaki was a Fulani scholar and commander who took up the jihad of Usman dan Fodio on the western frontier of the Kanem-Bornu Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahima-sori"><span class="enc-name">Ibrahima Sori</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrahima Sori, called Mawdo, 'the Great', was a Fulbe military leader of Futa Jallon in present-day Guinea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrium"><span class="enc-name">Ibrium</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibrium was a vizier of Ebla in northern Syria in the 24th century BCE, known from the city's royal archive.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibycus"><span class="enc-name">Ibycus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ibycus was a Greek lyric poet from Rhegion in southern Italy, active in the second half of the 6th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ica-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ica-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ica River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ica River flows from the Andes to the Pacific through the coastal desert of southern Peru, watering a valley around the city of Ica.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/icel"><span class="enc-name">Icel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Icel is the ancestor from whom the kings of Mercia traced their descent in Anglo-Saxon genealogies, which make him a descendant of the continental Anglian king Offa of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ichadon"><span class="enc-name">Ichadon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ichadon was a court official of Silla whose martyrdom in 527 led to the kingdom's acceptance of Buddhism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/icherisheher"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/icherisheher.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Icherisheher</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Icherisheher is the historic walled old city of Baku, Azerbaijan, on the shore of the Caspian Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ichijodani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ichijodani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ichijōdani</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ichijōdani is a narrow valley near Fukui in Japan, where the Asakura clan built their castle and town in the late 15th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ictinus"><span class="enc-name">Ictinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ictinus was a Greek architect of the fifth century BCE who designed the Parthenon on the Acropolis of Athens together with Callicrates, under the direction of Pericles…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ida-of-bernicia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ida-of-bernicia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ida of Bernicia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ida was the first known king of Bernicia in north-eastern England.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ida-maras"><span class="enc-name">Ida-Maras</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ida-Maras was a district of small kingdoms in the upper Khabur basin of north-eastern Syria, known chiefly from the Mari archives of the 18th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/idah"><span class="enc-name">Idah</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Idah is a town in Kogi State, Nigeria, on the east bank of the Niger.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/idalion-tablet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/idalion-tablet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Idalion Tablet</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Idalion Tablet is a bronze plaque of the early 5th century BCE from Idalion in Cyprus, inscribed in Greek in the Cypriot syllabary.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/idar-oberstein"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/idar-oberstein.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Idar-Oberstein</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Idar-Oberstein is a town on the Nahe in Rhineland-Palatinate, Germany, formed in 1933 by joining the towns of Idar and Oberstein.</span></a></li></ul>
